        "thinking": "I'm working out the body's joint coordinates, planning hip and leg positions with the foot targets and bone lengths needed for proper limb proportions.\n\nNow I'm figuring out the leg chain structure, deciding how to build the limb points, set the hip-to-ankle parenting, and choose radius values for the sweep so the leg tapers naturally from hip through knee to ankle, then placing foot plates using frame orientation along the sweep.\n\nSince the directional \"front\" convention along a downward-running tube is getting confusing, I'm switching to a ray-casting approach instead: define a helper that casts from a center point along an azimuth/elevation direction to find the surface hit, using the limb's chain position at a given height as the ray origin. I'm sketching out a `ringPlates` helper that iterates over t-values and azimuth angles to place plates around the limb surface at specific heights.\n\nI need plates to sit slightly embedded in the surface along the normal so they don't z-fight, offsetting the slab center outward by a quarter of its thickness so it's mostly above the surface but anchored into it. Now I'm working out plate dimensions for the limbs and sketching the torso loft surface with station widths at different heights for raycasting hits.\n\nI'm continuing to define torso stations up through the waist, chest, and trapezius, considering whether the loft should be box-sectioned or octagonal for more natural shoulder/chest shaping, and planning the loincloth plates and gold belt sweep that wraps the torso loop at the waist height.\n\nNow I'm sketching the sternum gem geometry—a scaled yellow icosahedron glowing at the chest—surrounded by a faceted gold torus cage with spikes, while double-checking the vertical alignment between hips, pelvis loft, and belt placement.\n\nMoving to the shoulders, I'm positioning the pauldron joints and domes relative to the torso width, defining a hexagonal lathe outline for each dome with sharp creased edges and layering a second lower tier for added bulk.\n\nStill writing dome tier function...\n\nI'll add a gold trim collar at the base of each tier plus obsidian spike caps on top, tilting the pauldron axis outward about 25 degrees, and attach it to the shoulder joint so it moves with the arm chain.\n\nFor the arm skeleton, I'm setting up shoulder-elbow-wrist chains parented to spine3, with finger digit chains hanging off each wrist joint. Now I need to compute the direction vector from elbow to wrist so I can properly orient the hand geometry.\n\nThe palm will be a frustum box extending from the wrist, with its width axis roughly aligned to the model's X direction since the forearm points down and outward. I'm sizing the hands fairly large and fist-like to fit the golem's bulky proportions, with four fingers per hand set to a partially curled, partially open rest pose rather than fully extended.\n\nWorking out finger positioning...\n\nWith palm facing inward toward the body, the hand's wide dimension runs front-to-back (Z) rather than side-to-side, so I'm swapping the palm box dimensions accordingly and spreading the fingers along Z from the base point.\n\nThe thumb attaches on the front-inner side of the palm, angling forward and down like a gorilla's thumb, with a bend partway through for the middle joint. I'm adding glowing orange cone tips at each fingertip for a bit of visual flair, and small hex plates over the knuckles for armor-like detail.\n\nNow I'm planning the forearm gauntlet: a thick hexagonal sweep tapering slightly, with four wrapping plates and gold collar bands at the wrist and elbow. I also want glowing magma-orange ball joints visible at the elbow, knees, shoulders (hidden under pauldrons), ankles, wrists, hips, and neck — low-poly icosahedra glowing between armor plates to suggest molten joints.\n\nFor the joint balls, I'm sizing them slightly larger than the limb radius so they bulge visibly through the plate gaps, and extending that same magma-crust motif to a glowing waist band between the chest and pelvis plates, with wider \"seam\" cracks painted into the torso surface to sell the cracked-crust look.\n\nSince flat paint alone can't glow, I'm combining a seam-painted crust texture (orange veins over dark rock, using cell-based noise to generate wider lava-vein seams rather than thin cracks) with separate emissive 3D glow geometry underneath for the strongest highlights. Still writing paint functions...\n\nChecking texel density tradeoffs: at 1024 resolution I'd get roughly 9mm/texel across the golem's ~60m² surface, while 2048 gives ~4.6mm, which justifies the larger atlas despite bake time costs — though I could also just shrink the painted area to save compute.\n\nDeciding on 2048 resolution since all parts (plates, limbs) are fully painted. For plate coloring, I'm defining a cel-shaded mottle pattern with lighter purple tones for lit areas, plus an edge-detection scheme using the normal dot product to distinguish face color from a lighter bevel edge color along the plate thickness.\n\nI'll create a separate paint per plate using the edge-detection trick, giving each plate a crisp readable rim while sharing the baked sheet; I'm also setting up a gold metal cel color and a set of flat emissive glow colors, noting that glowing surfaces must use flat colors rather than painted cel shading since they can't receive lighting detail.\n\nNow I'm designing flame plume cards for the back and head crest, placing frames along the upper back/neck pointing up and back with randomized variation, and generating roughly a dozen head-crown flame cards with slightly varied sizes to build a hair-like plume effect.\n\nI'm considering extra flame wisps at the pauldrons and fists but decide to keep the motif moderate—shoulders, head, and back only—skipping belt fire for scope. Since the glow cards ignore lighting anyway, card normals from the frame axis don't matter much for shading, and the default flow direction lets flames read well from front and back, so I'll enable cross-cards so they're also visible from the sides, plus scatter small ember sparks for extra detail.\n\nNow I'm planning Genshin-style tassels—a knotted gold cap with hanging red-orange threads—to hang from the belt plates, pauldron edges, and horns, placing roughly a dozen of them. I'm also weighing a ceremonial gold collar around the neck with a pendant gem instead of a sash or bandolier, then considering adding scattered obsidian spikes, small gravel/pebble details on the shoulders, and tapered lava-drip droplets hanging under the belt plates for extra surface texture.\n\nI'm deciding against adding tiny molten drip details at the fingertips and chin to save time, and moving on to defining the color palette—warm seam golds and oranges, cool purple basalt tones for the crust, dark lit/shadow obsidian pairs, and slightly lighter tones for the armor plates so they visually separate from the body.\n\nNow I'm working out the glowing eye colors (amber iris, orange rim, dark reddish pupil) and sketching the angled, aggressive almond eye shape in SVG.\n\nWriting SVG path...\n\nI need to make sure the inner corner of each eye tilts correctly toward the nose depending on which side it's mirrored to, since the creature's right eye appears on the viewer's left.\n\nStill writing eyebrow path coordinates...\n\nStill refining dimensions... now I'm planning how to apply the eye as a decal onto the mask surface, positioning and sizing it appropriately with mirroring for the opposite side.\n\nStill working out head geometry...\n\nContinuing the jaw box with frustum tapering forward, then adding tusk cones rising from the lower jaw's front corners and smaller upper teeth cones hanging down from the cranium's front edge, all positioned symmetrically along the side axis.\n\nI'll add an inner mouth glow box that's only visible when the jaw is open, then pose the jaw bone rotated forward around the X-axis by about 10-14 degrees so the mouth reads as slightly open -- building the jaw geometry first, then applying the pose so it carries the geometry with it.\n\nThen I'm sweeping a neck tube from the body up to the head's base, adding a gold collar ring around it, and placing curved horns on the sides of the head using a bezier sweep with tapering radius so they arc outward, up, and back, bringing the total figure height to around 3.25-3.4 once the flame crest above the head is included.\n\nNow I'm settling on an overall height of roughly 3.3 meters, confirming the torso-head proportions and pauldron tiers sit at good shoulder/ear levels, then working on a fiery mane plume along the upper back by ray-casting hits against the torso surface to get placement points, building frames tilted up-and-back so the flame cards lean outward like a mane.\n\nI'm sketching helper functions for spikes (with configurable base, direction, length, radius, and bone attachment) to use for obsidian shoulder crystals, then moving to the pelvis area to plan a loincloth of five hanging slab plates between the thighs, positioning the front panel and figuring out its tilt relative to the pelvis surface.\n\nI'm designing a generic tabard/plate function with a custom pointed outline shape, then adding a back tabard, hip plates, a gold belt loop with a hexagonal medallion buckle and gem, and hanging tassels. Now I'm starting to write the actual build code, setting up the builder, RNG seed, and skeleton structure before moving into the full geometry.\n\nBuilding spine, neck, head, and jaw chains...\n\nNow wiring up legs and arms off the spine, then starting the torso loft construction.\n\nBuilding leg geometry with hip, knee, ankle joints as a three-point chain, then sweeping a hexagonal cross-section along that path for the limb mesh...\n\nI'm now writing a jittered-path generator that interpolates points between two endpoints with random perpendicular displacement, then starting a crack-generation function that uses this